    "netmedex_hybrid_rag": "**Mechanistic chain from CFTR loss‑of‑function to inflammation in cystic‑fibrosis (CF)**  \n\n*Hop 1 – CFTR dysfunction → epithelial/mucus abnormalities*  \n- **Animal evidence**: CFTR‑deficient mice develop mucus accumulation, slowed gut motility, bacterial over‑growth and altered innate immunity, establishing a pro‑inflammatory milieu【PMID:24548777】.  \n- **Human/animal review**: The same triad of obstruction, infection and inflammation that follows CFTR loss in airways also operates in the intestine, confirming that CFTR is essential for normal GI ion transport and mucus clearance【PMID:23788646】.  \n\n*Hop 2 – Epithelial (and immune) CFTR loss → dysbiosis*  \n- **Mouse data**: Targeted deletion of cftr in epithelial cells reshapes the large‑intestinal microbiota, while cftr loss in immune cells drives dysbiosis in the small intestine, showing that CFTR activity directly shapes microbial communities【PMID:36289287】.  \n- **Human/animal synthesis**: Reviews attribute chronic gut dysbiosis in CF primarily to intrinsic CFTR dysfunction (alongside diet and antibiotics), underscoring CFTR as a root cause of microbial imbalance【PMID:35160099】.  \n\n*Hop 3 – Dysbiosis + mucus stasis → intestinal inflammation*  \n- **Mouse evidence**: CF mouse models with the above epithelial/immune defects exhibit heightened intestinal inflammation and altered innate immune responses, linking dysbiosis to inflammatory signaling【PMID:24548777】.  \n- **Human observations**: Even asymptomatic people with CF show chronic low‑grade intestinal inflammation and abnormal microbiota, indicating that the dysbiotic state translates into measurable inflammatory markers in patients【PMID:35160099】.  \n\n*Hop 4 – Gut inflammation → systemic (including pulmonary) inflammation*  \n- **Human inference**: Chronic intestinal inflammation in CF is proposed to act as a driver of systemic inflammation, which may exacerbate lung disease and other organ involvement【PMID:24548777】.  \n- **Cross‑organ context**: The gut‑lung axis literature notes that intestinal microbial and inflammatory changes can influence respiratory outcomes in CF, supporting a mechanistic link from gut inflammation to broader disease activity【PMID:34339302】.  \n\n**Overall inference** – The cited studies collectively map a stepwise pathway: loss of CFTR function perturbs epithelial ion transport → mucus retention and altered microbial habitats → dysbiosis → activation of innate immune pathways → chronic intestinal inflammation that can spill over to systemic inflammation and affect other CF‑related organs.  \n\n*Conclusion*: Empirical data from mouse models and human CF cohorts substantiate each link in the CFTR → mucus/dysbiosis → inflammation cascade, making the mechanistic path from CFTR loss to inflammation well supported."
